erial, 'O' Reserve only, 'X' No Required Textbook, 'Y' Required Course Material).\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_building_type_site\", \"desc\": \"Building classification columns replicated across the current and historical building tables of both table families: BUILDING_TYPE ('ACADEMIC', 'SERVICE', 'RESIDENT') and SITE ('MIT', 'BATES', 'LINC', 'BOS', etc.). They carry the same value domain but differ in time semantics: the building-table columns are the current classification, while the history-table columns are the classification recorded at each fiscal period, so filtering by one versus the other can select different building sets.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_total_units\", \"desc\": \"Total credit unit columns replicated across catalog and registrar subject tables. They represent the same business quantity (total credit units of a subject) but come from different processes and pair with different companion unit columns.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_fiscal_period\", \"desc\": \"Fiscal period code columns in YYYYMM format (e.g. '199602' = FY1996 period 2, '201505') appearing in two distinct roles: as the primary key of the month-grain time dimension (TIME_MONTH.FISCAL_PERIOD, also mirrored in TIME_DAY.FISCAL_PERIOD for day-to-month linkage), and as the snapshot-period stamp of the facilities historical tables (FCLT_BUILDING_HIST, FCLT_BUILDING_HIST_1, FCLT_BUILDING_ADDRESS_HIST, FCLT_FLOOR_HIST, FCLT_ROOMS_HIST, FCLT_MAJOR_USE_HIST). Both roles share the same YYYYMM value domain, but the time dimension uses it for calendar grouping while the history tables use it to mark when a facilities snapshot was taken.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_hass_attribute\", \"desc\": \"HASS (Humanities, Arts, and Social Sciences) attribute code and description columns. The code columns appear in the three course catalog feeds and in the CIS_HASS_ATTRIBUTE lookup; description columns exist in three variants: the catalog-embedded HASS_ATTRIBUTE_DESC, the lookup's DESCRIPTION_ON_FORM (registration-form wording), and DESCRIPTION_IN_BULLETIN (short bulletin wording). Selecting the right description variant depends on the wording requested by the question.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_department_name\", \"desc\": \"Department name columns across subject offering, catalog, lookup, employee, and library reserve instructor tables. Two academic naming conventions exist (DEPARTMENT_NAME vs OFFER_DEPT_NAME) with the same academic value domain; LIBRARY_COURSE_INSTRUCTOR.DEPARTMENT also holds academic departments but in a 'COURSE_NUMBER - Department Name' prefixed format (e.g. '18 - Mathematics'); EMPLOYEE_DIRECTORY.DEPARTMENT_NAME shares the column name but holds the HR organizational unit name (different value domain). Choosing the right one depends on the table family, the value format, whether the department is the catalog owner or the actual offering department, and whether an academic or HR department is meant.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_access_level\", \"desc\": \"Access level columns recorded at three granularity levels of the facilities domain plus the space-planning room feed: building-level ACCESS_LEVEL_CODE/ACCESS_LEVEL_NAME (current and per-fiscal-period history), floor-level ACCESS_LEVEL, and room-level ACCESS_LEVEL (FCLT_ROOMS, FAC_ROOMS, ZPM_ROOMS_LOAD). They share a numeric value domain (observed 0-3) but describe the access restriction of different physical objects or come from different feeds, so filters and joins must target the granularity and table family the question refers to.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_dlc_key\", \"desc\": \"Department/Lab/Center key columns (values like 'D_AEROASTRO', 'D_IDSS', 'D_BIOLOGY') identifying a DLC across the facilities, HR, space, and academic-department domains. They are the universal cross-domain join key linking facilities organizations and space units to HR organization units, the master department hierarchy, and academic departments. Naming and completeness vary: DLC_KEY in most tables, D_CODE in MASTER_DEPT_DCODE_PARENT, DLC_CODE in the master hierarchy tables; some columns carry composite values ('D_DSL:HQ') or nulls.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_building_room\", \"desc\": \"Combined building-room identifier columns (e.g. '1-080') appearing in both the space assignment fact table and the facilities room inventory. They identify the same physical room concept but come from different business processes with different coverage and companion measures.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_assignable_area\", \"desc\": \"Assignable-area measure columns at different granularity levels and time semantics: building-level current and per-fiscal-period assignable area, floor-level assignable area, and the space-domain assignable square footage. All FCLT_*/FAC_* columns are in square meters; BUILDINGS.BLDG_ASSIGNABLE_SQUARE_FOOTAGE is in square feet. Choosing the right column determines whether the measure is a building total, a floor total, a historical value, or a space-assignment figure.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_space_unit_key\", \"desc\": \"Space unit key columns identifying the organizational space-assignment unit across the space dimension and fact tables. SPACE_UNIT_KEY is the join key linking SPACE_DETAIL room rows to space unit identity (name/code) and, through SPACE_UNIT, to the owning facilities organization.\"}, {\"id\": \"column_concept_facility_organization_key\", \"desc\": \"Facility organization key columns attributing space units and rooms to organizations in the hierarchical organization tree. Two naming conventions exist across table families (FCLT_ORGANIZATION_KEY in FCLT_*/SPACE_* tables vs ORGANIZATION_KEY in FAC_* tables) with the same value domain.\"}]}}"
